    "q-121027": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121027",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$",
      "worked_solution": "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={630}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=30.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
