    "q-329544": {
      "dom_id": "step-q329544",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +4\\pi n,\\,x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $(-4,0].$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n, & \\, & x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=-\\frac{11}{6}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
